answer:
Plus, minus, asterisk, slash and percent. Asterisk multiplies, slash divides and gives a decimal, and percent gives the remainder after division.

code:
console.log(2 + 3, 7 - 2, 3 * 4, 10 / 4);
console.log('remainder:', 10 % 3, 10 % 2);
console.log('division always gives a decimal:', 6 / 3);
console.log('and it can go wrong:', 0 / 0);

real example:
Pagination checks are the classic use of percent. Page 3 of a 10 item list is 10 % 3 === 1, so a leftover row tells you the last page was short.

The plus operator changes job when one side is a string. It joins the two as text instead of adding them, and the number becomes text.

code:
console.log(2 + 3);
console.log('2' + 3);
console.log('items: ' + 3);
console.log('2' + 3 + 4);
console.log(2 + 3 + '4');

real example:
A log line reading count: plus a counter silently turns count into text, so a later numeric comparison on that log value fails.

Triple equals compares type and value with no conversion. Double equals converts one side first, so a text 10 matches the number 10.

code:
console.log('10' === 10);
console.log('10' == 10);
console.log(0 === false);
console.log(0 == false);
console.log(null === undefined);
console.log(null == undefined);

real example:
Assertions are comparisons, and the text 10 from a page is never strictly equal to the number 10 in your fixture. Convert one side or the test lies to you.

Because its conversion rules are hard to remember and the surprise always shows up in production data, not in the happy path. Strict equality has no rules to memorise.

code:
const cases = [[0, ''], [0, '0'], [0, false], [0, []], ['', []]];
cases.forEach(([a, b]) => console.log(JSON.stringify(a), '==', JSON.stringify(b), 'is', a == b));
console.log('with strict equality none of them match:');
cases.forEach(([a, b]) => console.log(JSON.stringify(a), '===', JSON.stringify(b), 'is', a === b));

real example:
A helper written with == passes on an empty field and a zero interchangeably, so the test stops telling you which one the bug is. Strict equality keeps the message honest.

Six values are falsy: false, 0, an empty string, null, undefined and NaN. Everything else is truthy, including the text 0 and a string of spaces.

code:
const falsy = [false, 0, '', null, undefined, NaN];
const truthy = ['0', ' ', 'false', [], {}, 42];
console.log('falsy count:', falsy.filter((v) => !v).length);
console.log('truthy count:', truthy.filter((v) => Boolean(v)).length);
console.log('the trap pair:', Boolean('0'), Boolean(0));

real example:
if (searchBox.value) never runs when the field holds the text 0. Guard on a length or trim test instead of on truthiness.

When the left side is falsy, && gives that falsy value back and never looks at the right side. The right side is only evaluated if it has to be.

code:
let calls = 0;
function probe() {
  calls++;
  return true;
}
const left = false;
console.log('result:', left && probe());
console.log('probe ran', calls, 'times');
const right = 'ready';
console.log('with a truthy left:', right && probe());
console.log('probe ran', calls, 'times');

real example:
user && user.getToken() avoids a crash on a null user because the right side never runs. That idiom exists because of short circuiting, and nothing else.

It returns one of the two values, never a boolean. If the left is falsy you get the left, otherwise you get the right.

code:
console.log('a' && 'b');
console.log(0 && 'b');
console.log('left' || 'right');
console.log('' || 'right');
console.log('so a chain can leak a value:');
const chain = 'title' && 'ready' && 42;
console.log(chain, typeof chain);

real example:
expect(page.title && 'ready') does not assert a boolean, it asserts that the value equals the truthy left side. The test passes or fails for reasons the author never intended.

It returns the left value when that value is falsy, otherwise the right one. So it is a fallback, and it cannot tell you whether the fallback was used.

code:
function label(name) {
  return name || 'guest';
}
console.log(label('Ana'), label(''), label(undefined));
const value = 0;
console.log('zero takes the fallback:', value || 10);
console.log('a ternary can see the zero:', value === 0 ? 'zero' : 'other');

real example:
A user name of 0 or an empty string takes the fallback with ||. If that difference matters, compare against undefined explicitly instead.

It only falls back for null and undefined. Zero, an empty string and false survive, which is exactly what a default value usually wants.

code:
const zero = 0;
const blank = '';
console.log('with or:', zero || 7, blank || 'default');
console.log('with nullish:', zero ?? 7, blank ?? 'default');
console.log('both agree on null:', null || 7, null ?? 7);
console.log('preferred in tests:', (zero ?? 7) === 0);

real example:
A retry count of zero is meaningful. A helper using || turns a deliberate no retries into five retries, so the test waits and then times out.

It stops the chain there and gives undefined instead of throwing. The whole chain becomes a short circuit.

code:
const response = { data: { user: null } };
console.log(response?.data?.user?.name);
console.log('normal access throws:');
try {
  console.log(response.data.user.name);
} catch (e) {
  console.log(e.name);
}
const missing = null;
console.log(missing?.deep?.deeper);

real example:
Optional chaining is the fix for a locator that may not be rendered yet. It returns undefined, so pair it with an explicit expect to make the wait visible.

It gives what is left after dividing, which is how you test for even numbers and how you wrap values. The sign of the result follows the left value, not the divisor.

code:
console.log(10 % 3, 10 % 2);
console.log('negative dividend keeps its sign:', -10 % 3);
console.log('wrapping an index:', 11 % 4);
console.log('and negative wrapping:', -1 % 4);
console.log('checking for even:', 4 % 2 === 0, 5 % 2 === 0);

real example:
Rotating through a list of browsers uses a positive modulo and a guard for negative steps, otherwise the first item in the cycle is wrong.

Two asterisks raise the left value to the power of the right one. It binds tighter than multiplication and works with negative and fractional exponents.

code:
console.log(2 ** 10);
console.log(2 ** 3 ** 2);
console.log('negative exponent:', 2 ** -2);
console.log('precedence vs multiply:', 3 * 2 ** 2);
console.log('in a helper:', Math.pow(2, 8) === 2 ** 8);

real example:
Bit shifts for a file size calculation are clearer with the exponentiation operator, and 2 ** 31 avoids the confusing 1 << 31 idiom in test helpers.

Both add one. Postfix hands back the old value and then updates the variable, prefix updates first and then hands back the new value.

code:
let a = 1;
const after = a++;
console.log(a, after);
let b = 1;
const before = ++b;
console.log(b, before);
let i = 0;
console.log(i++ + i++, i);
console.log('mixing them is a bad idea in a test');

real example:
A retry helper written as return retries++ returns the old count, so the first call reports 0 attempts even though it just made one.

They combine a logical test with an assignment in one step. The right side only runs when the left side needs to change, so nothing is computed for nothing.

code:
let cache = null;
cache ??= { id: 7 };
console.log('filled once:', cache.id);
cache ??= { id: 99 };
console.log('kept the first value:', cache.id);
let label = '';
label ||= 'fallback';
console.log(label);
let retries = 5;
retries &&= 3;
console.log('kept because truthy:', retries);
let skip = false;
skip &&= console.log('never printed');
console.log('skip is still', skip);

real example:
Setting up a lazy fixture with config ||= defaults avoids an unconditional second object literal, which matters when the default is expensive to build.

Not is a question about one value, so it answers yes or no. And and or are choices between two values, so they hand you back the value they picked.

code:
console.log(!0, !'', !null, !'0');
console.log('typeof the result:', typeof !0);
console.log('and or give values:');
console.log(typeof (0 && 'x'), typeof (1 || 'x'));
console.log('Boolean forces the answer:', Boolean(1 || 'x'));

real example:
if (!response.ok) works because not gives you true or false. Writing if (!(response.ok && retries > 0)) mixes both styles and is where the confusion starts.

It is a one line if and else. Read it as question then value if yes then value if no. Use an if when the two branches need statements, not just values.

code:
const retries = 3;
const label = retries > 0 ? 'will retry' : 'giving up';
console.log(label);
function describe(count) {
  let text;
  if (count === 0) {
    text = 'nothing ran';
  } else {
    console.log('logging side effect');
    text = count + ' ran';
  }
  return text;
}
console.log(describe(0));

real example:
Mapping a status code to a readable message in a test reporter is a good ternary. Building a request body with several properties is not, and reads better as an object literal.

Multiplication binds tighter than addition, so the multiplication happens first. Parentheses are the only reliable way to override it.

code:
console.log(2 + 3 * 4);
console.log((2 + 3) * 4);
console.log('unary minus binds tight:');
console.log(-3 * 2, 3 * -2);
console.log('not binds looser than arithmetic:');
console.log(!true + true);
console.log(typeof 1 + 1);

real example:
A price formula like total - discount * rate silently applies the discount to only part of the total if you forget the parentheses.

Addition runs left to right. The first version adds 1 and 2 as numbers, then meets a string and concatenates 33. The second version hits the string immediately and everything after it is text.

code:
console.log(1 + 2 + '3');
console.log(1 + '2' + 3);
console.log('both are text:', typeof (1 + 2 + '3'), typeof (1 + '2' + 3));
console.log('convert first to keep it numeric:');
console.log(1 + 2 + Number('3'));

real example:
Building an id like order- plus a counter plus a suffix drops the numeric add and silently changes what the id looks like.

It evaluates the left side, throws the value away, and returns the right side. It shows up in for loops to allow two update expressions, which is a readability trap.

code:
let a = (1, 2, 3);
console.log('only the last one survives:', a);
let i = 0;
let j = 0;
for (i = 0, j = 10; i < j; i++, j--) {}
console.log(i, j);
function sideEffect() {
  console.log('this ran');
  return 'ignored';
}
const value = (sideEffect(), 'done');
console.log('the return value is:', value);

real example:
A helper that relies on the comma operator to run two statements looks like it returns something it does not. Rewrite it as separate lines.

The in operator checks whether a key exists on an object. for...in walks the keys, for...of walks the values of anything iterable.

code:
const user = { name: 'Ana', role: 'admin' };
console.log('name' in user, 'age' in user);
const keys = [];
for (const key in user) keys.push(key);
console.log('for...in gives keys:', keys);
const values = [];
for (const value of Object.values(user)) values.push(value);
console.log('for...of gives values:', values);
const steps = ['login', 'logout'];
for (const step of steps) console.log('step', step);

real example:
Guarding a fixture with if ('id' in response) tells you a field is genuinely missing, which is stronger than checking its value for truthiness.

It throws the value away and always gives undefined. It was used to mark a javascript link as a placeholder so the page does not jump to the top.

code:
console.log(void 0);
console.log(void 'anything');
console.log(typeof void (() => 1));
const value = 42;
console.log('the value still exists:', value);
function stopDefault(event) {
  event && event.preventDefault();
  return undefined;
}
console.log('helper returns:', stopDefault(null));

real example:
Void is mostly a readability trap in test code. Any expression statement that returns something on its own line is better written as a plain assignment.

They convert each side to a 32 bit signed integer first. Anything past about two billion, or any decimal, gets truncated before the operator runs.

code:
console.log(5 & 3, 5 | 3, 5 ^ 3, ~5, 5 << 1, 5 >> 1);
console.log('decimals are chopped:', 5.9 & 3);
console.log('big numbers wrap:', 2147483647 + 1 | 0);
console.log('text is converted too:', '5' | 0);
console.log('do not use bitwise for ids:', '9007199254740993' | 0);

real example:
A quick parseInt replacement with the bitwise OR silently returns 0 for a large id, and the test then looks up a record that does not exist.

The bitwise operators convert text to a number first, then work on the binary bits. 5 is 101 and 2 is 010, so or-ing them gives 111, which is 7.

code:
console.log(5 | 2);
console.log('binary:', (5).toString(2), (2).toString(2), (5 | 2).toString(2));
console.log('and:', 5 & 3, (5).toString(2), (3).toString(2));
console.log('xor:', 5 ^ 1);
console.log('the safe way to parse:', Number('5') | 0, Number.parseInt('5', 10));

real example:
The idiom number | 0 was a popular fast integer cast. It is no longer faster than Number or parseInt and it breaks on decimals, so use those instead.

NaN means not a number, so it is not equal to anything including itself. Greater than and less than also fail because there is no ordering for a value that is not a number.

code:
const n = Number('abc');
console.log(n === n, n !== n);
console.log(n > 1, n < 1, n >= 1);
console.log('sorting puts NaN at the end:');
console.log([3, n, 1].sort((x, y) => x - y));
console.log('that is why you filter first:');
console.log([3, n, 1].filter(Number.isFinite).sort((x, y) => x - y));

real example:
Sorting expected durations with a comparator that hits NaN leaves the order unchanged, so the diff report lists the failures in a random order.

An empty array is truthy, so the not operator turns it into false. Then an empty array is loosely equal to false, so the whole expression is true.

code:
const list = [];
console.log(list == !list);
console.log('the steps:', Boolean(list), !list, list == false);
console.log('but strict equality says no:', list === false);
console.log('with a real array:');
const rows = [1];
console.log(rows == !rows);

real example:
This is the clearest demonstration that == has conversion rules nobody remembers. Rewrite the check as an explicit length test and it becomes obvious.

Loose equality converts the empty string to a number, and an empty string converts to zero. Strict equality compares types first and stops there.

code:
console.log('' == 0, '' === 0);
console.log('the conversions:', Number(''), Number('0'), Number(' '));
console.log('null too:', null == 0, null === 0, null == false);
console.log('undefined:', undefined == 0);
console.log('the safe emptiness test:');
const input = '';
console.log(input.trim() === '');

real example:
A guard written as if (value) to detect an empty field also fires for 0 and false. Explicitly compare the trimmed string to an empty string instead.

Once a string appears, plus concatenates from that point on. The earlier numbers were already added, so the order of the operands changes the result.

code:
console.log('a' + 1 + true);
console.log(1 + true + 'a');
console.log('count: ' + 3 + 4);
console.log(3 + 4 + ' count');
console.log('null joins as text:', 'value: ' + null);
console.log('undefined too:', 'value: ' + undefined);

real example:
Asserting on a log line built with plus and two placeholders gives you one number and a mess. Use template literals so every value is clearly text.

Decide what you actually mean. If you mean both are absent, compare for null or undefined. If you mean same value, use triple equals, and handle NaN separately.

code:
function sameValue(a, b) {
  return a === b || (Number.isNaN(a) && Number.isNaN(b));
}
console.log(sameValue(0, 0), sameValue('', ''), sameValue(null, null));
console.log('no more surprises:', sameValue(0, ''), sameValue(0, '0'));
function bothAbsent(v) {
  return v === null || v === undefined;
}
console.log(bothAbsent(null), bothAbsent(0), bothAbsent(''));

real example:
A generic deep equal helper with == passes for 0 and an empty string and fails for two NaNs. Strict equality plus a NaN check is the version worth shipping.

Strict equality on objects compares the reference, not the contents. Two objects are only equal if they are literally the same object in memory.

code:
const expected = { id: 7, tags: ['a'] };
const actual = { id: 7, tags: ['a'] };
console.log('strict:', expected === actual);
console.log('but contents match:', JSON.stringify(expected) === JSON.stringify(actual));
const shared = expected;
console.log('same reference:', shared === expected);
const copy = { ...expected };
console.log('a spread copy:', copy === expected);

real example:
expect(response.body).toEqual(expected) in Playwright does a deep comparison, which is why it passes while a triple equals on the same objects fails.

Use Math.trunc, Math.floor, or parseInt with an explicit radix. They handle large numbers and decimals the way you actually mean.

code:
const values = [5.9, -5.9, '42.7px', 9007199254740993];
console.log('bitwise:', values.map((v) => v | 0));
console.log('trunc:', values.map((v) => Math.trunc(Number(v))));
console.log('floor:', Math.floor(5.9), Math.floor(-5.9));
console.log('parseInt with radix:', Number.parseInt('42.7', 10));

real example:
Reading a scroll offset or a pixel position from the page and truncating it with a bitwise operator gives a negative number for small offsets on some browsers.

Mixing the nullish operator with and or without parentheses is a SyntaxError, because the language will not guess which one you meant.

code:
const count = 0;
const label = count ?? (count || 'unknown');
console.log(label);
// uncommenting the next line is a SyntaxError for the whole file
// const broken = count ?? count || 'unknown';
const wrapped = (count || count) ?? 'unknown';
console.log('parentheses fix it:', wrapped);

real example:
A test file that mixes a nullish fallback with a truthy fallback will not parse at all, so the runner reports a syntax error instead of a failing test.

And returns the left value when it is falsy, so a legitimate zero gets thrown away and you get the default instead of the value you passed.

code:
function withAnd(retries) {
  return retries && 3;
}
function withNullish(retries) {
  return retries ?? 3;
}
console.log('and:', withAnd(0), withAnd(5));
console.log('nullish:', withNullish(0), withNullish(5));
console.log('same for booleans:', false && true, false ?? true);
console.log('truthy fallback still fine with or:', withAnd(1));

real example:
A retry helper written with and returns three for every input, including zero retries, so the test never actually checks the no-retry path.

Put parentheses around whichever call comes first. The nullish fallback should go outside when zero is a value you want to keep.

code:
const count = 0;
console.log('nullish first:', count ?? (count || 'none'));
console.log('or first, parenthesised:', (count || count) ?? 'none');
console.log('and here both give none:', 0 ?? (0 || 'none'));
const missing = null;
console.log('null case:', missing ?? (missing || 'none'));
console.log('clean rule: value ?? default, and use || only for blank strings');

real example:
Reading a count from the page with a fallback needs the nullish version, while reading a label with a blank fallback needs the or version. Mixing them needs the parentheses.

Null converts to zero for a comparison, so null is not greater than zero but is greater than or equal to zero. Undefined converts to NaN, which fails every comparison.

code:
console.log(null > 0, null >= 0, null > -1);
console.log(undefined > 0, undefined >= 0);
console.log('mixed:', null > undefined, null == undefined);
console.log('the readable version:');
const limit = null;
console.log(limit === null || limit === undefined ? 'no limit' : limit);

real example:
Checking an optional timeout with if (timeout > 0) silently passes when the timeout is null, so a missing timeout looks like a huge one.

Date has a toString method, so the plus sign uses it and joins the text. That is why date plus anything becomes text and stops being usable as a date.

code:
const due = new Date('2024-05-01T00:00:00Z');
console.log('with a string:', due + '!');
console.log('with a number:', due + 1);
console.log('to be a date again:', new Date('2024-05-01') + 1);
const ms = new Date('2024-05-01T00:00:00Z').getTime();
console.log('arithmetic on the timestamp works:', ms + 1);

real example:
Comparing a due date read from the API against a fixture date with plus builds a string, so the comparison silently becomes a text comparison.

Multiplication, division and subtraction force a numeric conversion, which gives NaN for text that is not a number. The result is NaN and nothing warns you.

code:
const total = '19.99';
console.log(total * 2);
console.log(total / 2);
console.log('this is the fix:');
console.log(Number(total) * 2);
console.log('and the guard:', Number.isNaN(Number(total)) ? 'bad value' : 'ok');

real example:
A tax helper that multiplies a price read from the page by a rate returns NaN, and the failing assertion shows NaN instead of pointing at the missing Number call.

A non-empty string is truthy whatever it contains, so not gives false. To test for emptiness, check the length or compare against an empty string.

code:
const input = '0';
console.log('not is:', !input);
console.log('what you probably wanted:', input === '');
console.log('empty check:', input.length === 0);
console.log('blank check:', input.trim().length === 0);
console.log('and for numbers:', !0, 0 === 0);

real example:
A validation helper built on not value accepts the text 0 as a filled field and rejects the number 0. Use a trim length test on strings.

It gives NaN, because a fractional power of a negative number has no real answer. Math.pow throws in that case while the operator just gives NaN.

code:
console.log((-8) ** (1 / 3));
console.log(Math.pow(-8, 1 / 3));
console.log('integer power is fine:', (-8) ** 3);
console.log('guard before you compute:');
const value = -8;
console.log(Number.isInteger(value) ? Math.sign(value) * Math.abs(value) ** (1 / 3) : 'NaN path');

real example:
A helper that computes a cube root for a test fixture returns NaN on negative inputs, and the report shows NaN rather than the value that caused it.
